    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d257">

                <pb facs="0402" n="286" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg286"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 839.00/1701.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Minister Sullivan to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegrams.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Santo Domingo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-05-12T10:00:00-04:40">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 12, 1915—10 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your May 10, 6 p.m. Ovendo’s band defeated and scattered. Dominican Government
                    vigilant and taking rigorous measures. Although on the surface peace reigns
                    activities of the Government has created tenseness and the President
                    confidentially informs me cartridges exhausted and urges point made by him in my
                    telegram of April 24, 11 a.m. Request for munitions instead of money would seem
                    to indicate sincerity and believe it should be granted.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sullivan.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
